<h2>Structure of a NASM Program</h2>

<p>NASM is line-based. Most programs consist of <dfn>directives</dfn> follo=
wed by one or more <dfn>sections</dfn>. Lines can have an optional <dfn>lab=
el</dfn>. Most lines have an <dfn>instruction</dfn> followed by zero or mor=
e <dfn>operands</dfn>.

</p><p><img src=3D"https://cs.lmu.edu/~ray/images/nasmstructure.png" alt=3D=
"nasmstructure.png"></p>
<p>Generally, you put code in a section called <code>.text</code> and your =
constant data in a section called <code>.data</code>.

</p><h2>Details</h2>

<p>NASM is an awesome assembler, but assembly language is complex. You need=
 more than a tutorial. You need details. Lots of details. Be ready to consu=
lt:
</p><ul>
<li><a href=3D"http://www.nasm.us/doc/">The NASM Manual</a>, which is prett=
y good!
</li><li><a href=3D"http://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/processors/archi=
tectures-software-developer-manuals.html">The Intel Processor Manuals</a>
</li></ul>

