<p>In macOS land, C functions (or any function that is exported from one mo=
dule to another, really) must be prefixed with underscores. The call stack =
must be aligned on a 16-byte boundary (more on this later). And when access=
ing named variables, a <code>rel</code> prefix is required.

</p><h2>Understanding Calling Conventions</h2>

<p>How did we know the argument to <code>puts</code> was supposed to go in =
<code>RDI</code>?
Answer: there are a number of conventions that are followed regarding calls=
.

</p><p>When writing code for 64-bit Linux that integrates with a C library,=
 you must follow the
calling conventions explained in the
<a href=3D"http://www.x86-64.org/documentation/abi.pdf">AMD64 ABI Reference=
</a>.
You can also get this information from
<a href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86_calling_conventions#x86-64_Call=
ing_Conventions">Wikipedia</a>.
The most important points are:
</p><ul class=3D"spaced">
<li>From left to right, pass as many parameters as will fit in registers. T=
he order in which
registers are allocated, are:
  <ul class=3D"compressed">
    <li>For integers and pointers, <code>rdi</code>, <code>rsi</code>, <cod=
e>rdx</code>,
        <code>rcx</code>, <code>r8</code>, <code>r9</code>.
    </li><li>For floating-point (float, double), <code>xmm0</code>, <code>x=
mm1</code>, <code>xmm2</code>,
        <code>xmm3</code>, <code>xmm4</code>, <code>xmm5</code>, <code>xmm6=
</code>, <code>xmm7</code>.
  </li></ul>
</li><li>Additional parameters are pushed on the stack, right to left, and
are to be <em>removed by the caller</em> after the call.</li>
<li>After the parameters are pushed, the call instruction is
made, so when the called function gets control, the return
address is at <code>[rsp]</code>, the first memory parameter is at
<code>[rsp+8]</code>, etc.
</li><li><strong>The stack pointer <code>rsp</code> must be aligned to a 16=
-byte boundary
before making a call</strong>. Fine, but the process of making a call pushe=
s the return address (8 bytes) on
the stack, so when a function gets control, <code>rsp</code> is not aligned=
. You have to make
that extra space yourself, by pushing something or subtracting 8 from <code=
>rsp</code>.
</li><li>The only registers that the called function is required to preserv=
e (the calle-save registers)
are: <code>rbp</code>, <code>rbx</code>, <code>r12</code>, <code>r13</code>=
, <code>r14</code>, <code>r15</code>.
All others are free to be changed by the called function.
</li><li>The callee is also supposed to save the control bits of the XMCSR =
and the x87 control word,
but x87 instructions are rare in 64-bit code so you probably don=E2=80=99t =
have to worry about this.
</li><li>Integers are returned in <code>rax</code> or <code>rdx:rax</code>,=
 and floating point values are returned
in <code>xmm0</code> or <code>xmm1:xmm0</code>.
</li></ul>

<p>Got that?  No?  What=E2=80=99s need is more examples, and practice.

</p><p>Here is a program that illustrates how registers have to be saved an=
d restored:

</p><div class=3D"filename"><span>fib.asm</span></div><pre><code class=3D"l=
anguage-x86asm hljs" data-highlighted=3D"yes"><span class=3D"hljs-comment">=
; -------------------------------------------------------------------------=
----</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; A 64-bit Linux application that writes the f=
irst 90 Fibonacci numbers. To</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; assemble and run:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">;     nasm -felf64 fib.asm &amp;&amp; gcc fib.=
o &amp;&amp; ./a.out</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; --------------------------------------------=
---------------------------------</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">global</span>  main
        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">extern</span>  printf

        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">section</span> .text
<span class=3D"hljs-symbol">main:</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">push</span>    <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rbx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; we hav=
e to save this since we use it</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">ecx</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">90</span>                 <s=
pan class=3D"hljs-comment">; ecx will countdown to 0</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">xor</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rax</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rax</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; rax will hold the current number</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">xor</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rbx</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rbx</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; rbx will hold the next number</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">inc</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rbx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; rbx is=
 originally 1</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-symbol">print:</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; We need to call printf, but we are u=
sing rax, rbx, and rcx.  printf</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; may destroy rax and rcx so we will s=
ave these before the call and</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; restore them afterwards.</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">push</span>    <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rax</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; caller=
-save register</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">push</span>    <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rcx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; caller=
-save register</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rdi</span>, format             <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; set 1s=
t parameter (format)</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rsi</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rax</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; set 2nd parameter (current_number)</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">xor</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rax</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rax</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; because printf is varargs</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; Stack is already aligned because we =
pushed three 8 byte registers</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">call</span>    printf                 =
 <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; printf(format, current_number)</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">pop</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rcx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; restor=
e caller-save register</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">pop</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rax</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; restor=
e caller-save register</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rdx</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rax</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; save the current number</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rax</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rbx</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; next number is now current</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">add</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rbx</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">rdx</span>                =
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; get the new next number</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">dec</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">ecx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; count =
down</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">jnz</span>     print                  =
 <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; if not done counting, do some more</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">pop</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rbx</span>                     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; restor=
e rbx before returning</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ret</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-symbol">format:</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">db</span>  <span class=3D"hljs-string=
">"%20ld"</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">10</span>, <span class=3D"hlj=
s-number">0</span>
</code></pre>
