wd">rax</span><span class=3D"pln">, [</span><span class=3D"kwd">rsp</span><=
span class=3D"pln">+8]<br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; add &nbsp; &nbsp; </s=
pan><span class=3D"kwd">rax</span><span class=3D"pln">, </span><span class=
=3D"kwd">rsi</span><span class=3D"pln"><br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; ret<=
/span></pre>

<p>If our function were to make another call, you would have to adjust rsp =
to get out of the way
at that time.

</p><p>On Windows you can=E2=80=99t use this scheme because if an interrupt=
 were to occur, everything above the stack pointer gets plastered. This doe=
sn=E2=80=99t happen on most other operating systems because there is a <dfn=
>red zone</dfn> of 128 bytes past the stack pointer which is safe from thes=
e things. In this case, you can make room on the stack immediately:

</p><pre class=3D"prettyprint lang-gas"><span class=3D"pln">example</span><=
span class=3D"pun">:</span><span class=3D"pln"><br>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nb=
sp; </span><span class=3D"kwd">sub</span><span class=3D"pln"> rsp</span><sp=
an class=3D"pun">,</span><span class=3D"pln"> </span><span class=3D"lit">24=
</span></pre>

<p>so our stack looks like this:

</p><div class=3D"memory-view">
<div>rsp</div><div>a</div>
<div>rsp+8</div><div>b</div>
<div>rsp+16</div><div>c</div>
<div>rsp+24</div><div>retaddr</div>
