<h2>Using NASM on Windows</h2>

<p>I=E2=80=99m not sure what the system calls are on Windows, but I do know=
 that if you want to assemble and link with the C library, you have to unde=
rstand <a href=3D"https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/7kcdt6fy.aspx">t=
he x64 conventions</a>. Read them. You will learn such things as:
</p><ul>
<li>The first four integer parameters are passed in RCX, RDX, R8, and R9. T=
he rest are to be pushed on the stack.
</li><li>The callee must preserve RBX, RBP, RDI, RSI, RSP, R12, R13, R14, a=
nd R15.
</li><li>The first four floating point parameters are passed in, you guesse=
d it, XMM0, XMM1, XMM2, and XMM3.
</li><li>Return values go in RAX or XMM0.
</li></ul>

<p><b>IMPORTANT</b>: There=E2=80=99s one thing that=E2=80=99s really hard t=
o find in any documentation: the x64 calling convention requires you to all=
ocate 32 bytes of <a href=3D"http://stackoverflow.com/a/30191127/831878">sh=
adow space</a> before each call, and remove it after your call. This means =
your =E2=80=9Chello world=E2=80=9D program looks like this:

</p><div class=3D"filename"><span>hello.asm</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"language-x86asm hljs" data-highlighted=3D"yes"><span class=3D"hljs-comment=
">; -----------------------------------------------------------------------=
-----------------</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; This is a Win64 console program that writes =
"Hello" on one line and then exits.  It</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; uses puts from the C library.  To assemble a=
nd run:</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">;</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">;     nasm -fwin64 hello.asm &amp;&amp; gcc he=
llo.obj &amp;&amp; a</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-comment">; --------------------------------------------=
--------------------------------------------</span>

        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">global</span>  main
        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">extern</span>  puts
        <span class=3D"hljs-meta">section</span> .text
<span class=3D"hljs-symbol">main:</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">sub</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rsp</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">28h</span>                  =
      <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; Reserve the shadow space</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">mov</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rcx</span>, message                    <span class=3D"hljs-comment">=
; First argument is address of message</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">call</span>    puts                   =
         <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; puts(message)</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">add</span>     <span class=3D"hljs-bui=
lt_in">rsp</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">28h</span>                  =
      <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; Remove shadow space</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-keyword">ret</span>
<span class=3D"hljs-symbol">message:</span>
        <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">db</span>      <span class=3D"hljs-st=
ring">'Hello'</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>                 =
     <span class=3D"hljs-comment">; C strings need a zero byte at the end</=
span>
</code></pre>
<p>Did you notice we actually reserved 40 bytes? Thirty-two bytes of shadow=
 space is a minimum requirement. In our <code>main</code> function, we are =
calling another function, so our stack <a href=3D"https://docs.microsoft.co=
